"Combines an actual value, a target marker, and qualitative reference ranges on one compact scale.\n\nUse when: A single metric needs comparison against an explicit target. Several comparable metrics must fit in aligned dashboard rows.\n\nAvoid when: Reference bands lack defensible meanings or the metric is a distribution.\n\nVisual grammar: Bar length encodes actual performance from zero. A perpendicular marker encodes the target; background intervals encode defined performance ranges.\n\nBuild: Define units, target, and ordered reference thresholds before drawing. Lay neutral reference bands beneath a narrower actual bar. Add a clear target tick and label actual and target values outside the bands.\n\nCheck: Check the target and actual use the same period and denominator. Verify bands cover the displayed domain without contradictory overlaps. Use shared scales only when rows measure compatible quantities.\n\nPitfalls: Qualitative bands can falsely imply objective standards if thresholds are unexplained. Avoid traffic-light colors as the sole meaning of the ranges.\n\nExample: Show synthetic weekly output of 82 units against a 90-unit target over labeled reference bands.\n\nAlternatives: Bar chart, Error bar chart, Dot plot."
